---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import InstallLine from '../components/InstallLine.astro';
import { CODE_FUNCTIONS, SURFACES, HOWTOS } from '../data/catalog.mjs';
import { cell, SURFACE_SAMPLES } from '../data/examples.mjs';

const tabs = [
  { label: 'Bash', alias: 'Bash', surface: 'shell' },
  { label: 'Python', alias: 'Python', surface: 'python' },
  { label: 'TypeScript', alias: 'TypeScript', surface: 'typescript' },
  { label: 'Ruby', alias: 'Ruby', surface: 'ruby' },
  { label: 'R', alias: 'R', surface: 'r' },
  { label: 'Rust', alias: 'Rust', surface: 'rust' },
  { label: 'SQL', alias: 'SQL', surface: 'duckdb' },
];

const bash = [cell('filter', 'shell').runs[0]];
const surfaceOf = (slug) => SURFACES.find((s) => s.slug === slug);
const sixHowtos = HOWTOS.slice(0, 6);
---
<Base title="ThinkThen">
  <h1>Code that knows what you mean.</h1>
  <p class="lede">Simple functions that give your software the judgment to handle whatever comes its way. Hand one a message, ask "Is this a complaint?", and get back yes, no, or not sure.</p>

  <p>Your software already branches on an exit code, a pattern, or a column. Now it can branch on a decision about meaning, in one line. No regex that breaks by Friday. No chat model wrapped in a prompt, a parser, a retry loop, and a bill.</p>

  <p><code>decide</code> is an if that understands. <code>filter</code> is a grep that understands. <code>rank</code> is a sort that understands. The rest are listed below. Every answer comes with a probability, and you set the bar. Anything in the middle comes back as not sure, and your software sends it to a person. In Bash the exit code is 0 for yes, 1 for no, and 3 for not sure.</p>

  <div class="tabbed" data-fallback="Bash">
    <div class="tabs" role="tablist">
      {tabs.map((t) => (
        <button type="button" role="tab" data-lang={t.label} data-alias={t.alias} aria-selected={t.label === 'Bash'}>{t.label}</button>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="cell" data-lang="Bash">
      {bash.map((run) => <Terminal run={run} caption="filter keeps the two complaints." />)}
      <InstallLine line={surfaceOf('shell').install[0][0]} caption="Install the command" />
    </div>
    {tabs.slice(1).map((t) => (
      <div class="cell" data-lang={t.label} hidden>
        <Code code={SURFACE_SAMPLES[t.surface].code} />
        <InstallLine line={surfaceOf(t.surface).install[0][0]} caption={`Install it for ${surfaceOf(t.surface).name}`} />
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/install/">Every language, database, and backend</a></p>

  <h2>The ten functions</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {CODE_FUNCTIONS.map((fn) => (
      <a class="tile" href={`/functions/${fn.name}/`}>
        <b>{fn.name}</b>
        <span>{fn.line}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/functions/question-file/">Save a question as a file</a></p>

  <h2>Learn it on the Beatles</h2>
  <p>Jev makes the decisions. It is a System One model from TypeSafe. It answers in one step, writes no text, and needs no training. <a href="/learn/beatles-bench/">Beatles Bench</a> shows it answering questions about 306 Beatles songs. Every answer there is known ahead of time. Each page takes one slide from the talk and one command. Or start with the <a href="/learn/tutorial/">tutorial</a>.</p>

  <h2>Jobs your software can take on</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {sixHowtos.map((h) => (
      <a class="tile" href={`/how-tos/${h.slug}/`}>
        <b>{h.title}</b>
        <span>{h.reader}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/how-tos/">See every how-to</a></p>
</Base>
